# Everest Trailways — environment variables
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development. `.env.local` is gitignored;
# this file is committed and must never hold a real value. `pnpm scan:secrets`
# runs before every commit and fails if it does.
#
# In production these are set in Vercel under
# Project → Settings → Environment Variables.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Durable rate limiting and the daily spend ceiling.       REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHAT TO CONFIGURE IN VERCEL: add the Upstash Redis integration from the Vercel
# Marketplace and link it to this project — it writes KV_REST_API_URL and
# KV_REST_API_TOKEN itself, and nothing else is needed.
#
# Without these the site works and the matcher still answers, but it answers
# from the deterministic fallback and NEVER calls the model — because a spend
# we cannot count is a spend we do not make. See src/lib/spend.ts.
#
# Upstash's own variable names are read too, if you provision it directly.
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The trek matcher's assistant path.                                   OPTIONAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Without it the section still works: /api/match falls through to the
# deterministic matcher in src/lib/matcher-fallback.ts, which runs the same
# questions against the same dataset and applies the same refusals.
#
# Server-side only. It is never read from a client component — check:security
# walks the import graph from every "use client" module and fails the build if
# it ever is.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Content-Security-Policy switches.                                    OPTIONAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# CSP_STRICT=1 uses the nonce-based policy with no script unsafe-inline.
# It REQUIRES every page to be dynamically rendered — without that it blocks
# every script on the site. Read the long comment in src/lib/security-headers.ts
# before setting it.
# CSP_STRICT=

# CSP_REPORT_ONLY=1 demotes the policy to Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only.
# For diagnosing a policy problem in production. It protects nobody while set.
# CSP_REPORT_ONLY=
